include(".../Telecoms/Telecoms.jl")
using .Telecoms

##########
Utility:

# CSV
Telecoms.save_as_csv(data, display_ratio)
Where data is a 2D vector of anything, express this as a CSV and
save it to filename to which the function appends ".csv". Set 
kwarg "quiet" to true to suppress loading bar. v[1][1], v[1][2]...
kwarg "maxfilesize" exits if proposed file size exceeds this value
in MB.

Telecoms.load_csv(filename)
kwarg "header" will attempt to add header to output. If false, it
will ignore header. This function can only load CSVs of consistent
data type, namely Float64 or string - a column at the second
row cannot be parsed to Float64, the column will be loaded as a
string.

# Binary floats
Use these to save space when saving data.

Telecoms.save_as_binary_floats(t, x, filename)
Unlike CSV this is just for waveform data with uniform
t and variable x. ".dat" is appended to filename.

Telecoms.save_as_binary_floats(x, filename)
Variable x is saved to binary file.

Telecoms.load_binary_floats_waveform(filename)
Loads the CSV as with save_as_binary_floats(t, x, filename).

Telecoms.load_binary_floats(filename)

# Binary words - OMA
OMA binary format uses even less space, but resulting floats only have 
65535 discrete values. It's a result of the OMA quantisation depth.
It isn't really useful in storing the result of offline calculations,
so there is no way to save to this file type.
Telecoms.OMA_channel_n_data among others stores this type.

Telecoms.load_binary_words(filename)
Telecoms.load_OMA_words(filename)
OMA and old OMA both use this format. Equivalent functions are
load_floats_from_binary and load_OMA.

# Legacy formats
Telecoms.load_mathcad(filename)
As from the TDS520C floppy "Mathcad" format.

# DAQ2
Load the output from Ali Masoudi's first DAS box.
load_DAQ2(dirname,
    n_files,
    fast_range,
    decimation,
    slow_avg,
    fast_avg,
    fastslow_avg,
    memory_limit,
    axes)
return ret
return ret, distance, time (if axes)
Fast = distance (x) axis, slow = time (y) axis.
Where dirname is the name of the directory in which DAS data is stored.
The memory limit may be set to prevent
too much data from being loaded in. The default memory limit is 80% of the
total system memory.
An averaging/decimation algorithm is selected by setting one of decimation,
slow_avg, fast_avg, fastslow_avg to an undersampling factor N where every
N+1 sample is dropped in the case of decimation and averaged in the case
of avg. A value of N=0, the default, means no algorithm should be used.
The fast_range is a tuple which can be set to load in the desired
distance sample range. 
Additionally, decimation skips decimation samples in
distance. The spatial average stores the mean of the next fast_avg
samples in the closest sample. The temporal average stores mean of next
slow_avg samples is stored in the earliest sample. fastslow_avg^2 samples'
mean is stored in the closet and earliest sample.

print_loading_bar(n1, n2)
Print a loading bar on the same line according to the quotient of the
two arguments.

