Frequency analysis:

Arguments are an array of Numbers.

The FFT-type functions operate on complex-valued time-domain data.
These correspond to double-sideband (DSB) spectra.

The RFFT-type functions operate on real-valued 
time-domain data.
These correspond to single-sideband (SSB) spectra.

# FFT
Functions:
Telecoms.fft(x, Fs) or (i, q, Fs)::(w, X)
Compute FFT with sample rate used to generate the frequency axis.

Telecoms.fft_norm
FFT with output amplitude normalised to the number of samples.

Telecoms.fft_magnitude
Output is magnitude of FFT.

Telecoms.fft_magnitude_dB
Output is 20log10(FFT).

Telecoms.fft_magnitude_norm
Telecoms.fft_magnitude_norm_dB
Telecoms.fft_phase
Output is the phase of FFT.

# RFFT
Telecoms.rfft(x, Fs)::(w, X)
Real-input FFT with the frequency axis starting at 0 Hz.

Telecoms.rfft_norm
Telecoms.rfft_magnitude
Telecoms.rfft_magnitude_dB
Telecoms.rfft_magnitude_norm
Telecoms.rfft_magnitude_norm_dB
Telecoms.rfft_phase

# PSD
Telecoms.psd(x, fs)::(f, PSD)
Compute the single-sided PSD of a real-valued signal using the
Fourier transform. The output is expressed in dB.
2*RFFT(X)/(N*dt)

Telecoms.psd_linear

Telecoms.psd_double
Double-sided PSD.

Telecoms.psd_double_linear

# IFFT
Telecoms.ifft(X, fs)::(t, x)
Compute IFFT with sample rate used to generate a time axis starting
at t=0.

